{"summary":"Revised `draft-02` only as requested: Scene 1 now ends on the path and match-cuts into R0; Scene 2 adds grounded early-game settlement and Moss’s naming; portals use seamless destination views; narration is restrained and third-person; and Scene 12 replaces the deliberate anchor death with a falling creeper. Internal revision, spoken-pass, pronunciation and QC records are updated. The unlocked Story plan still needs later alignment with the revised R0 sequence and death mechanism.","document":"# Production script — The Portal That Nobody Made\n\n> Draft ID: `draft-02`. This revision follows the current unlocked echo-reality Story proposal and the latest script note. It records no creative lock or production validation.\n\n## Status and use\n\n- Editorial target: approximately 20 minutes. Scene timings are planning guides, not measured results or structural quotas.\n- `R0` to `R4` are crew labels and are never spoken.\n- One creator-voiced, third-person narrator follows the protagonist’s understanding. The on-screen protagonist does not speak.\n- Crying-obsidian portal behaviour is invented story canon. Seamless portal views are planned with the Immersive Portals mod; compatibility and capture behaviour have not been verified for the selected Minecraft version.\n- Minecraft fire, wolf, creeper and charged-anchor staging must be tested before capture.\n\n## Continuity keys\n\n- **MATCH PATH** is the same camera position, lens, path and direction of travel in Scenes 1 and 2. R4 shows the later smoke and developed landscape; R0 shows the fresh clearing.\n- Every ravine contains the same lightning-shaped granite seam. It is the first orientation anchor in each echo.\n- The protagonist’s survey journal begins mostly blank, with a stitched repair on one corner and a three-line ravine symbol. The Weeping Atlas is a weathered counterpart with the same repair, symbol and handwriting.\n- For crew continuity, the wolf is called Moss throughout. No in-world name appears above him until the Scene 2 name-tag beat.\n- Moss’s rug is moss-green wool. Its final added square sits nearest the open hearth and provides the proposed fire path.\n- Echo portals use crying obsidian, directional tear-like particles and a low layered hum. Their centres show seamless views of the destination rather than opaque purple fields.\n- The conventional Nether portal used for supply journeys has ordinary obsidian and familiar audio.\n- Moss’s R4 fate remains unseen. Show no body, collar drop or death message.\n- The final falling-creeper route needs a believable dark ledge above the activation position. The portal response to its blast reaching the charged frame is invented canon, not a claimed vanilla mechanic.\n\n---\n\n### 1. EXT. R4 RIDGE / HOMESTEAD PATH — NIGHT\n\n*Editorial target: 0:18*\n\n**Visual intent.** Open on smoke, urgent movement and unexplained purple cargo. End on a path composition that can jump directly to the same place in R0.\n\n**Audio intent.** Wind, hurried movement and distant fire. No narration and no music before the jump cut.\n\n**Action**\n\nNight. The protagonist crests the ridge from the direction of a conventional Nether portal. Crying obsidian and glowstone fill his pack and hands.\n\nHe sees smoke before he sees the house.\n\nHe drops the purple cargo and runs.\n\nFrom **MATCH PATH**, the camera watches him follow the path towards the smoke. He runs away from camera towards the homestead.\n\nJump cut on his movement into Scene 2.\n\n**Production note.** Preserve the exact camera position, lens, path line and direction of travel for Scene 2. Scene 10 resumes the emergency from the end of this shot.\n\n---\n\n### 2. EXT. R0 CLEARING ABOVE THE RAVINE — MORNING / PASSAGE OF DAYS\n\n*Editorial target: 2:45*\n\n**Visual intent.** Use the match cut to replace the later emergency with an ordinary arrival. Establish the protagonist and wolf through recognisable early-game routines before the portal interrupts them.\n\n**Audio intent.** The distant R4 fire cuts to morning wind, leaves and footsteps. Let work sounds carry the montage. Introduce the portal hum only near the end.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe jump cut keeps the exact **MATCH PATH** camera position. Morning replaces night. The developed R4 landscape is now the untouched R0 clearing, with no house or smoke.\n\nOn the same path, the protagonist walks towards the clearing with the wolf beside him. His equipment is simpler and his journal is mostly blank.\n\nTitle over the matched shot: **THE PORTAL THAT NOBODY MADE**\n\nAt the overlook, in a second locked composition marked **MATCH FRAME A**, the wolf checks the grass and sits. The protagonist unfolds a map whose explored edge ends at the ridge. He opens the clean survey journal, showing its stitched corner, and draws the three-line ravine symbol.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The map ended at the ridge. There were no paths or torches beyond it, and no one else used the world.\n\nThe wolf remains in the best patch of sun.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The wolf sat down first. That was vote enough. They could stay.\n\n**EARLY-GAME MONTAGE — SEVERAL MINECRAFT DAYS**\n\n- The protagonist cuts oak and birch, makes basic tools and raises a small temporary shelter.\n- At night, he kills a zombie and a skeleton near the unfinished walls, then clears a spider from the crop edge.\n- He plants wheat and later uses it to lead two cows into a simple fenced pen.\n- He fishes from the ravine stream. Ordinary catches fill a small chest.\n- On a later cast, a name tag comes up on the line.\n- After gathering enough iron for an anvil, he labels the tag **MOSS** and uses it on the wolf. The name appears above him for the first time.\n- The shelter gains a bed, a chest, a furnace and a small roof. The protagonist records the clearing and starter build in his journal.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The first days were ordinary. He cut wood, put up a roof, planted wheat and dealt with whatever reached the clearing after dark.\n\nThe newly named Moss follows him back from the anvil.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The tag said Moss. It suited him.\n\nHold on the compact shelter, cow pen, crops and fishing place. It is a beginning, not yet the later R4 house.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> A bed, a pen and a roof were enough for him to start calling the clearing home.\n\nWhile the protagonist marks a tree near the ravine edge, a low hum rises from below. Moss turns his head first. The protagonist stops cutting and listens.\n\nHe checks the cave area on his map. The surface is now familiar; the space beneath it remains blank.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> He knew the clearing by then. He had never opened the cave beneath it, and nothing he had built made that sound.\n\nHe packs the journal and starts down the ravine. Moss follows.\n\n**Production note.** Save **MATCH FRAME A**, including camera, lens, sun direction, equipment, Moss position and journal pose, for Scene 13. The fishing and name-tag beats are planned capture actions, not recorded survival or probability tests.\n\n---\n\n### 3. INT. R0 RAVINE CAVE — DAY\n\n*Editorial target: 1:35*\n\n**Visual intent.** Make the first sight of the portal both an authorship contradiction and a visible destination. The Rain Echo should already be clear through the frame.\n\n**Audio intent.** Let mining and paw sounds recede beneath the unfamiliar portal hum. Rain should be audible through the opening before the protagonist crosses.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe protagonist descends by water and short block placements. Moss waits at a narrow ledge until the route is safe.\n\nA lightning-shaped granite seam cuts across the cave wall.\n\nBeyond it stands a complete crying-obsidian frame. Tear-like particles move consistently towards its far face.\n\nThe centre is not an opaque violet surface. It is a seamless view into what first appears to be a mirror of the same cave, except rain falls through an opening in the ceiling on the other side while the R0 stone around the protagonist remains dry.\n\nThe camera moves laterally. The far view shifts with natural parallax. There is no reflected protagonist: the opening shows a real, rain-soaked version of the cave.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> It was crying obsidian, but it was not any portal he knew how to make. The opening already showed where it went.\n\nThe identical granite seam is visible beyond the frame. Rainwater crosses it and feeds a waterfall that does not exist behind the protagonist.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> Same cave. Same stone. Rain on the other side.\n\nThe protagonist circles the frame and checks the ceiling, floor and adjoining tunnels. There is no access shaft, scaffold, camp, dropped inventory or mining trail.\n\nHe compares the coordinates with his journal. The cave lies beneath the clearing he has now surveyed and settled, but he never opened a route to it.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> No one else used the world. There was no route into the cave, but somebody had placed every block.\n\nMoss approaches the opening, then looks back at the protagonist.\n\nThe protagonist checks the frame once more, removes Moss’s wait command and brings him beside the threshold.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> He checked it twice, brought Moss beside him and walked through.\n\nThey cross in one continuous movement. Dry cave audio gives way to rain as the camera passes the threshold.\n\n**Production note.** The planned Immersive Portals presentation must preserve the matched destination view and camera parallax. Mod version, multiplayer or replay compatibility, wolf transfer and capture stability remain unverified.\n\n---\n\n### 4. INT./EXT. R1 RAIN ECHO — DAY\n\n*Editorial target: 1:35*\n\n**Visual intent.** Confirm that the destination matches what was visible through the portal. Keep the geography recognisable while changing weather, growth and player history.\n\n**Audio intent.** Continue the rain already heard through the opening. Keep the same portal hum beneath it.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe protagonist and Moss complete their step into the Rain Echo beside the same lightning-shaped granite seam.\n\nRainwater pours through the cave roof. A waterfall occupies the route that was dry. Dense vegetation covers the surface. The R0 shelter, cow pen and surface marker are absent.\n\nThe protagonist checks his coordinates twice and overlays a sketch of the granite seam with the one in his journal.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The coordinates matched. The weather, water and tree growth did not.\n\nAt the surface, the ravine profile and distant tree line remain recognisable beneath their different growth.\n\nBack at the frame, the opening now shows a dry version of the cave. From this angle, the surface and its missing build are out of sight. The view resembles the R0 cave he left.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> From the wet side, the opening looked into a dry cave again. With the surface out of view, it looked like the way back.\n\nThe protagonist places one cobblestone block against the left foot of the R1 frame and draws its position in the journal.\n\nHe and Moss step through the dry view.\n\nThe camera crosses with them. On the far side, grey ash drifts through still air.\n\n---\n\n### 5. INT./EXT. R2 ASH ECHO TO R3 ARCHIVE ECHO — LATE DAY\n\n*Editorial target: 1:35*\n\n**Visual intent.** Turn the mistaken return into a practical test. The absent marks must remain readable without narration.\n\n**Audio intent.** Replace rain with dry wind and distant falling debris. Use a short musical pulse only when the second missing mark confirms the pattern.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe protagonist turns towards the left foot of the frame. The cobblestone block is absent.\n\nAt the surface, a naturally scorched tree line stands above an exposed ravine shelf. None of the R0 construction is present.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> He had stepped towards the dry cave he knew. The world beyond it was not his.\n\nHe searches the ground and checks the journal sketch. He then constructs a more deliberate mark: two cobblestone blocks in an L, with a torch on the outer block. He records the arrangement and remaining block count.\n\nThe portal opening shows a darker version of the cave fitted with an old survey recess. Supplies are thinning, and there is no safer route across the damaged surface.\n\nThe protagonist and Moss cross.\n\nIn the Archive Echo, the L-shaped mark is absent from the matching frame foot.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> One missing block could have been damage. Two different marks missing at the same coordinates were enough.\n\nHe writes a single word beneath the sketches: **ECHOES**.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The places shared a layout, but not a history. Each crossing had moved them to another one.\n\n**Production note.** Use identical close framing for both marks and both absences. Do not rely on colour grading alone to distinguish R1, R2 and R3.\n\n---\n\n### 6. INT. R3 ARCHIVE ECHO — DUSK\n\n*Editorial target: 2:00*\n\n**Visual intent.** Let the Atlas organise evidence already seen. Its final activation notes should remain incomplete and practical rather than becoming a lore lecture.\n\n**Audio intent.** Quiet the score for readable inserts. The seamless destination image begins stable, then skips and darkens as the mouth fails.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe survey recess is old but recognisably arranged by someone with the protagonist’s habits: torches on the same side, compact storage and the three-line ravine symbol cut into a desk.\n\nInside a protected drawer lies a weathered journal with the same stitched corner as the protagonist’s book.\n\nHe places the journals together. The shorthand, ravine symbol and arrow-shaped number seven match.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The repair and handwriting matched his journal. The pages did not.\n\n**ATLAS INSERTS — HOLD EACH LONG ENOUGH TO READ**\n\n1. Several plans of the same ravine laid over one another.\n2. A chain of portal mouths with every unanchored arrow pointing forwards: **NEXT MOUTH. NEVER PREVIOUS.**\n3. The phrase **ONE FRAME, MANY MOUTHS.**\n4. A complete crying-obsidian frame connected to a charged respawn anchor, with an ignition mark and a keyed arrow towards **FIRST THRESHOLD**.\n5. A note beside the receiving mouth: **UNKEYED ENTRY CONTINUES FORWARDS.**\n6. The warning **BUILDER PRESENT AT IGNITION BECOMES THE KEY.**\n7. A stone baffle and blast-distance diagram whose final measurement has been torn away.\n\nThe protagonist compares the Atlas arrows with the particles and his two absent marks.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The first diagrams matched what he had tested. An unkeyed crossing went to the next mouth, never the previous one.\n\nHe turns to the complete-frame diagram.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The last diagram was less certain. A complete frame, a charged anchor and its builder present at ignition could point towards the first threshold. The page with the safe distance was torn.\n\nThe view through the portal skips. One layer of the hum drops out. Gravel falls into the old surface stair and more shifts when the protagonist tries to clear it.\n\nMoss backs towards the frame. The protagonist looks from the blocked stair to the failing opening, takes both journals and chooses the remaining route.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The stair was blocked and the opening was failing. Forward was the only route still open.\n\nThey cross as the destination image contracts around them.\n\n**Production note.** The Atlas wording is the maximum required exposition. Do not add further portal explanation unless a later animatic demonstrates a specific comprehension failure.\n\n---\n\n### 7. INT./EXT. R4 HOME ECHO — MORNING\n\n*Editorial target: 1:15*\n\n**Visual intent.** Make arrival feel useful before revealing that the route has closed. Change the immediate objective from travelling to surviving.\n\n**Audio intent.** Begin with birds and stillness. Let the opening collapse softly rather than explode.\n\n**Action**\n\nThey emerge into a dry, habitable version of the ravine. The granite seam remains.\n\nBehind them, the destination image breaks apart. The projected upper blocks fade, leaving an incomplete set of crying blocks around empty air.\n\nThe protagonist tries ordinary fire. It burns briefly and goes out without opening a view.\n\nHe compares the broken mouth with the Atlas’s complete frame. He lacks the required crying obsidian, charged anchor and cover.\n\nAt the surface, there is clean water, timber and open ground. He plants a marker directly above the dead mouth.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> For a moment, the quiet was useful.\n\nHe checks the supplies remaining in his pack and begins a small shelter before nightfall.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The mouth had closed. He did not have the blocks or anchor to rebuild it, so he put shelter first.\n\n---\n\n### 8. EXT./INT. R4 HOMESTEAD — PASSAGE OF TIME\n\n*Editorial target: 2:30*\n\n**Visual intent.** Earn the R4 house and Moss’s importance through shared routines. Build the proposed material cause of the fire in plain sight.\n\n**Audio intent.** Use a restrained theme assembled from work sounds: block placement, furnace ticks, crops, rain and Moss’s movement. Leave room for quiet domestic holds.\n\n**Action — MONTAGE IN REPEATED LOCKED COMPOSITIONS**\n\n- The first shelter gains a floor, walls and a compact upper storey.\n- Moss repeatedly stands on the exact block the protagonist intends to place. The protagonist waits and leaves it until last.\n- Moss waits at a bridge while it is made safe, follows across and receives food after minor damage.\n- The protagonist builds a stone-backed open fireplace in the shared room.\n- Moss chooses the bare floor beside it. The protagonist lays a small moss-green wool rug beneath him.\n- During a storm, Moss goes directly to the rug while thunder sounds outside.\n- The protagonist keeps his current survey journal and the weathered Atlas side by side. His pages fill with echo diagrams and material counts.\n- A conventional obsidian Nether portal is built away from the ravine. Across several journeys, the protagonist returns with small amounts of crying obsidian and glowstone.\n- At the dead mouth, he measures space for a complete frame and sketches a stone baffle, but lights nothing.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> He called it a camp while the walls were temporary.\n\nThe house gains crops, storage and signs of use.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> Then the crops came in, Moss knew which doorway opened first, and the bed stayed where it was. It had become a house.\n\nMoss occupies a construction block. The protagonist lowers his tool and waits.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> Moss often stood on the next block. He learnt to place that one last.\n\nAt the hearth, Moss lies with his front paws beyond the rug’s edge. The protagonist adds one square of moss-green wool between the old rug and the warmth.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> Moss always chose the warm edge. He added one more square of rug.\n\nHold on the new wool square near the open flame. Stone surrounds the hearth, but wool now provides a proposed path towards the timber floor and supports.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The fireplace was stone-backed. He checked the stone and missed how close the wool now sat to the flame.\n\nThe protagonist checks a material diagram. Only the final crying blocks and glowstone remain uncollected.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> He gathered the Atlas materials a little at a time. The frame could wait until he had enough cover.\n\n**Production note.** The final rug extension is the proposed causal change. Its distance and route into timber require version-specific testing before the set is approved.\n\n---\n\n### 9. INT. R4 HOMESTEAD / CONVENTIONAL PORTAL — DAY\n\n*Editorial target: 1:10*\n\n**Visual intent.** Make leaving Moss an established safety decision. End on the unnoticed material consequence.\n\n**Audio intent.** Keep the domestic theme warm but spare. After the conventional portal closes, isolate the hearth crackle.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe protagonist packs for the final supply journey. Moss stands to follow.\n\nHe checks the stone hearth, feeds Moss and gives the sit command on the green rug. Moss settles on the newly added square.\n\nThe protagonist leaves through the conventional obsidian portal.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The final run was for the last crying blocks and enough glowstone to charge the anchor. After that, he planned to measure again before lighting anything.\n\nMoss remains beside the hearth.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> Moss came on ordinary journeys. The Nether was not one of them. The house looked safer.\n\nThe conventional portal sound fades.\n\nHold on the open hearth and nearest wool square. Fire appears on its edge. Moss remains sitting as the first flame spreads out of frame.\n\nCut before the room is engulfed.\n\n**Production note.** Do not imply this spread has been verified. If the selected version cannot reproduce the planned wool-to-timber route, revise the geometry with human approval while preserving the visible domestic cause.\n\n---\n\n### 10. EXT./INT. R4 HOMESTEAD AND SEARCH ROUTES — NIGHT TO DAWN\n\n*Editorial target: 2:35*\n\n**Visual intent.** Resume the emergency exactly where Scene 1 stopped. Reveal an empty sitting place rather than a graphic answer, then let the failed search accelerate the frame decision.\n\n**Audio intent.** Continue Scene 1’s wind, footsteps and fire. After the wall breaks, suppress music and use distant fire, footsteps and pauses for possible returning paws.\n\n**Action**\n\nResume the **MATCH PATH** run from Scene 1. The protagonist follows the path to the burning house.\n\nA storage window breaks outward. He passes recoverable chests without looking at them.\n\nAt the shared-room doorway, flame has reached the moss-green wool beside the stone fireplace. Moss is not visible.\n\nThe protagonist empties his water bucket through the doorway. Steam fills the opening, but burning timber falls across the direct route.\n\nHe turns, breaks through the shortest plank wall and enters through the smoke.\n\nThe shared room is empty. The stone fireplace remains, but the green rug has burned away. A charred fan leads from its position into the timber floor and wall. Moss’s sitting place is bare.\n\nShow no body, collar drop or message.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The hearth remained. The rug did not. The burn started where the extra square had been and travelled into the timber.\n\nThe protagonist moves the dropped crying obsidian and glowstone out of the fire path, then searches.\n\nHe checks behind surviving furniture, the crop field, ridge, conventional portal shelter and ravine route. He carries Moss’s food and stops repeatedly to listen.\n\nAt the storm shelter beneath the window, he finds nothing.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> He checked the crops, the ridge, the ravine path and the place Moss used during storms. Nothing answered.\n\nA long still hold. Only fire and morning birds remain.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> There was no collar, no message and no proof of what had happened. Moss was simply gone.\n\nAt dawn, the protagonist returns to the ruins. He sees the recovered crying obsidian beside the weathered Atlas.\n\nHe opens the page marked **FIRST THRESHOLD**. A small drawing of Moss appears beside the clean R0 journal sketch.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The Atlas marked one route as the first threshold. It was the last place where he had seen Moss alive.\n\nHe looks towards the ravine.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> He could keep searching or spend more time testing the frame. By morning, he had decided to finish it.\n\nHe takes the Atlas, his journal, the crying obsidian and the glowstone. The ruined house continues to burn behind him as he descends.\n\n---\n\n### 11. INT. R4 RAVINE — DAY TO NIGHT\n\n*Editorial target: 1:45*\n\n**Visual intent.** Answer the construction mystery through physical work. Establish the partial cover, ignition position and overhead route needed for the creeper death.\n\n**Audio intent.** Build rhythm from block placement and stone work. Introduce the original portal hum only as a faint memory in the score.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe protagonist uses the granite seam, surface marker and coordinates to locate the dead mouth exactly.\n\nHe fills its missing positions and constructs a complete crying-obsidian frame. Its dimensions and block imperfections match the R0 portal.\n\nHe places a respawn anchor according to the Atlas diagram and charges it with glowstone. The frame remains dark.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The granite seam and coordinates placed the dead mouth exactly where the Atlas said it would be.\n\nHe places the final crying block.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The frame was made from blocks he had gathered. Only its destination was unusual.\n\n**ATLAS INSERT**\n\nThe keyed arrow runs from the full frame to **FIRST THRESHOLD**. At the far mouth, the unkeyed arrow continues into the ordinary forward chain.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> If the diagram was right, lighting this frame would project the mouth beneath the first clearing. His earlier crossing would still continue forwards because he had entered without the builder’s key.\n\nThe protagonist builds a thick stone baffle offset from the frame. He tests whether he can reach its inside edge with flint and steel while remaining behind most of the cover.\n\nThe torn safety page removes the final distance measurement.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The builder had to be present at ignition. The page showed cover, but the measurement that set it back from the frame was missing.\n\nHe adds the thickest blocks that still permit him to reach the opening.\n\nThe floor and frame are lit with torches. High behind the working position, a recessed shelf remains dark and partly hidden by the ravine wall.\n\nNight settles above the ravine. The protagonist puts both journals behind the baffle, equips flint and steel and faces the frame.\n\n**Production note.** The dark shelf and drop line must make the creeper’s arrival spatially plausible without turning it into an early reveal. Validate safe capture blocking separately from the story outcome.\n\n---\n\n### 12. INT. R4 RAVINE — NIGHT\n\n*Editorial target: 1:05*\n\n**Visual intent.** Let an ordinary Minecraft hazard interrupt the planned ignition. Keep the protagonist facing the frame, then withhold the impact visually and continue after his death.\n\n**Audio intent.** Remove the score before ignition. The falling-creeper landing, close hiss and explosion carry the death. Afterward, use muffled silence, debris and the gradual portal hum.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe protagonist checks the Atlas drawing of the first threshold, the charged anchor and the baffle.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The frame was complete. The anchor was charged. The torn diagram gave him no better measurement.\n\nA brief visual memory: Moss in the R0 sun, Moss occupying a construction block and Moss on the rug.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> At the first threshold, Moss was still beside him. That was enough for him to try.\n\nThe protagonist steps into the position that lets him reach the frame and raises the flint and steel.\n\nA heavy landing sounds on the stone directly behind him. The close ignition hiss of a creeper follows at once.\n\nHe does not turn around.\n\nHe closes his eyes.\n\nFade to black.\n\nThe creeper explodes.\n\nNo narration follows his death.\n\nFor several seconds, nothing happens.\n\nThe picture returns to a locked exterior plate. Smoke and falling debris fill the ravine. The crying frame still stands. The protagonist is absent and his equipped items are scattered.\n\nThe charged anchor gives one brief violet flash inside the smoke.\n\nOne tear-like particle crosses the frame. Then another. Purple light gathers along the crying blocks, and the empty centre resolves into a clear, seamless view of the corresponding R0 cave a fraction too late.\n\nThe original low hum returns.\n\n**Production note.** The creeper fall, fatal blast, item scatter, surviving frame, anchor flash and delayed destination view may require separate controlled plates. A creeper blast activating the charged echo frame is invented story canon and has not been demonstrated as vanilla behaviour.\n\n---\n\n### 13. THROUGH THE FRAME / R0 CLEARING — MORNING\n\n*Editorial target: 1:00*\n\n**Visual intent.** Reveal the apparent beginning as the destination. The seamless portal view should connect R4 to R0 before the repeated surface composition changes its meaning.\n\n**Audio intent.** Carry the portal hum through the crossing, then reuse the exact Scene 2 narration take and morning ambience.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe camera remains after the protagonist’s death. It approaches the active R4 frame as omniscient audience access, not as his viewpoint or surviving consciousness.\n\nThe R0 cave is already visible through the opening. The camera crosses into it, follows the matching granite seam and rises through stylised layers of dark stone and soil.\n\nIt reaches the surface in **MATCH FRAME A**.\n\nMorning. The earlier protagonist opens his mostly blank survey journal. Its stitched corner and three-line ravine symbol are clear. Moss sits at the overlook, before receiving his on-screen name tag later in Scene 2.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O. — IDENTICAL RECORDING FROM SCENE 2)**\n\n> The wolf sat down first. That was vote enough. They could stay.\n\nThe low portal hum rises from beneath them.\n\nThe protagonist pauses and turns towards the ravine.\n\nCut underground to the complete active crying-obsidian mouth waiting in the cave. Through its seamless centre, rain falls in R1.\n\nCut to black.\n\n**Production note.** The through-ground move may use a purpose-built vertical set and matched dissolves. It must not imply that the dead protagonist or his consciousness crosses the portal.\n\n**End.**\n","files":[{"path":".studio/internal/script/revision-pass-status.json","content":"{\n  \"schema_version\": 2,\n  \"draft_id\": \"draft-02\",\n  \"passes\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"promise\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Scenes 1–3 now connect the future smoke to an ordinary R0 arrival, establish early survival life with Moss and reveal the rainy destination through the impossible portal before entry. Scenes 4–13 retain the cumulative echo evidence, domestic loss, final construction and opening destination.\",\n      \"decision\": \"For draft-02, retain the matched path cut, early-game settlement, seamless rain reveal and delayed builder pay-off without adding an explanatory character.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"causality\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Settlement makes the underground hum interrupt ordinary play; the visible rainy destination causes investigation; a dry cave view makes the attempted return reasonable; marker failures make the Atlas credible; the failed mouth causes settlement; material gathering causes Moss to remain home; the rug supplies the proposed fire path; loss accelerates frame completion; and the falling creeper causes the fatal blast that reaches the charged frame before its delayed activation.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Retain these links and validate the seamless sightlines, rug fire, creeper fall, blast result and fictional charged-frame response before capture.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"dialogue_and_subtext\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"A textual revision changed every narrator passage to restrained third-person language, kept the protagonist silent and retained concise Atlas labels. No spoken performance evidence is claimed.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Keep the narrator external but close to the protagonist’s available understanding. Test breath, natural delivery and dramatic restraint in the pending spoken pass.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"visual_storytelling\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The matched path, early-game actions, on-screen Moss name tag, seamless rainy view, granite seam, absent marks, paired journals, rug extension, dropped cargo, falling-creeper sound beat, frame geometry and repeated clearing composition carry the principal information visually.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Use locked match shots and readable destination views; do not rely on narration or colour grading alone to distinguish echoes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"production_feasibility\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The textual feasibility pass retains one avatar, one wolf and one narrator; adds a bounded early-game montage; plans seamless views through Immersive Portals; and permits controlled plates for wolf crossings, fire, the creeper fall, blast aftermath and final transition.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Proceed to storyboard and version-specific tests before capture. Treat mod compatibility, name-tag capture, fire spread, wolf transfer, creeper blocking and final activation as unverified.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"continuity_and_canon\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Draft-02 consistently uses third-person narration, introduces Moss’s on-screen name in R0, presents every active echo mouth as a seamless destination view and attributes the protagonist’s death to a falling creeper. The R0–R4 chain, Weeping Atlas, R4 rug fire and R0 destination remain intact.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Treat the latest human script note as governing this unlocked draft. Record that the unlocked Story document still describes the earlier R0 arrival and fatal direct-anchor activation and will need separate alignment if approved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"character_agency\",\n      \"required\": false,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The protagonist chooses the clearing, establishes a survival home, investigates the hum, enters with Moss, tests successive echoes, settles in R4, prepares the frame gradually and decides to attempt ignition after Moss disappears. The creeper, rather than a deliberate self-detonation, causes his death.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Preserve his agency in choosing and preparing the attempt, but do not describe the creeper death as a chosen suicide or successful personal crossing.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"escalation\",\n      \"required\": false,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Ordinary settlement becomes an impossible visible destination, then displacement, failed return, distinct echoes, incomplete knowledge and entrapment. Domestic stability becomes loss, and the final technical attempt is interrupted by an ordinary hostile-mob hazard.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Keep each echo tied to a changed understanding and keep the creeper beat brief rather than expanding it into a separate threat sequence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"theme_and_change\",\n      \"required\": false,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The protagonist moves from routine survival and evidence gathering towards a hurried attempt after Moss’s disappearance. The revised climax ends in an accidental Minecraft death while the technical route still opens too late.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Keep the narration grounded and avoid turning the accident into a spoken moral or implying that the portal restores Moss.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"information\",\n      \"required\": false,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The rainy destination is visible before entry; limited cave sightlines make the dry return view plausibly misleading; absent marks establish separate echoes; Atlas diagrams clarify keyed and unkeyed travel; Scene 11 establishes the dark overhead route; and the blast aftermath confirms death before the R0 view appears.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Retain the unkeyed-entry note and ensure the seamless view always depicts the destination actually reached.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"scene_necessity\",\n      \"required\": false,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Scene 1 now supplies the future emergency and match cut only. Scene 2 establishes settlement, early gameplay and Moss’s name before the portal. The remaining scenes retain their existing functions, while Scenes 11–12 now prepare and deliver the falling-creeper outcome.\",\n      \"decision\": \"If timing grows after a spoken or animatic pass, compress individual montage shots without removing the requested woodcutting, mob combat, cows, fishing, name tag or Moss-naming beats.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"audience_results_plan\",\n      \"required\": false,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The QC record defines questions for the new match cut, early-game montage, seamless destination comprehension, third-person narration and creeper interruption. No audience result or causal claim is recorded.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Use future audience evidence to form hypotheses, then inspect the corresponding edit and comments before proposing changes.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n"},{"path":".studio/internal/script/table-read-notes.md","content":"# Table-read / spoken-pass notes — The Portal That Nobody Made\n\n## Method and participants\n\nNo solo spoken read, multi-voice table read, storyboard read or animatic review has been conducted or supplied for `draft-02`.\n\nParticipants: none recorded.\n\nPlanned evidence: a solo spoken read for breath, tone and provisional duration, followed by a storyboard or animatic review focused on the Scene 1–2 match cut, seamless portal sightlines, echo geography and final activation logic.\n\n## Clarity failures\n\nNo observed spoken-read failures can be recorded yet.\n\nItems to test:\n\n- whether the same-camera jump from the R4 run to the R0 walk is immediately readable;\n- whether viewers understand that the wolf is the same companion before and after the on-screen Moss name tag;\n- whether the early-game montage feels like settlement rather than an unrelated gameplay compilation;\n- whether the rainy view through the first frame clearly represents the destination before entry;\n- whether the restricted cave view makes the mistaken return to a dry echo reasonable;\n- whether R1, R2 and R3 remain distinct without crew labels;\n- whether viewers understand that the conventional Nether portal is unrelated to the crying frame;\n- whether keyed R4-to-R0 travel and unkeyed R0-to-R1 travel remain understandable;\n- whether the falling-creeper audio, fade and aftermath clearly communicate the protagonist’s death;\n- whether Moss’s R4 fate reads as intentionally unknown rather than accidentally omitted.\n\n## Rhythm, breath, and repetitive dialogue\n\nNo performance evidence is available.\n\nTest whether the expanded Scene 2 montage delays the portal beyond the intended audience promise. Listen especially to the Atlas explanation in Scene 6, the route explanation in Scene 11 and the search narration in Scene 10.\n\nThe narration should sound observational and grounded. Flag any delivery that restores the heightened first-person tone removed in this revision.\n\n## Emotional transitions\n\nNo performed transition has been assessed.\n\nTest the movement from the smoke run into the calm R0 walk, the naming of Moss, the later R4 domestic montage, the pause after the failed search and the abrupt creeper interruption. The final death should read as an ordinary hazard interrupting a consequential attempt, not as a deliberate self-detonation.\n\n## Geography and visual comprehension\n\nNo storyboard or animatic evidence is available.\n\nThe review should track **MATCH PATH**, **MATCH FRAME A**, the lightning-shaped granite seam, portal orientation, destination visible through each frame, left-foot marks, surface marker and vertical relationship between cave and clearing.\n\nConfirm that the view shown through each seamless portal matches the echo reached after crossing. Use a simple route map during review if any transition depends on narration for orientation.\n\n## Exposition and subtext\n\nNo spoken exposition pass has occurred.\n\nCheck whether third-person references remain unambiguous, particularly before Moss receives his name. Verify that narration does not repeat information already visible through the portals or in the Atlas inserts.\n\nThe Atlas labels are intentionally concise. The creeper should not require advance narration; the dark overhead route and sound cue should provide the necessary spatial cause.\n\n## Production difficulty exposed by the read\n\nA read has not exposed production difficulty.\n\nKnown script-level risks to examine during storyboard and capture planning are the exact path match, early-game montage length, fishing and name-tag staging, Immersive Portals compatibility, wolf portal blocking, five readable ravine states, reproducible fire geometry, the falling-creeper route, blast aftermath and final through-ground transition.\n\n## Changes made and unresolved decisions\n\nNo changes are attributed to a table read or spoken pass.\n\nThis revision implements the supplied note by ending Scene 1 on the path, matching it to the R0 arrival, expanding Scene 2 with ordinary early gameplay and Moss’s naming, showing destinations seamlessly through the crying frames, changing narration to restrained third person and replacing the direct-anchor death with a falling creeper.\n\nUnresolved evidence includes the target Minecraft edition and version, compatible Immersive Portals version, validated portal sightlines, wolf transfer, name-tag capture method, fire geometry, creeper fall and damage blocking, fictional charged-frame activation, and any duration trims identified by the pending read.\n"},{"path":".studio/internal/script/pronunciation-guide.md","content":"# Pronunciation and language guide\n\n- Delivery accent: creator’s natural English; do not imitate an accent.\n- Spelling convention: British English.\n- Narration perspective: external third person, kept close to what the protagonist can observe or infer.\n- Delivery tone: grounded and conversational. Do not add heightened drama that is absent from the wording.\n- These cues support consistency only; the creator’s natural, intelligible delivery takes priority.\n\n## Names and terms\n\n- **Moss** — /mɒs/; short vowel, like the plant.\n- **ravine** — /rə-VEE-n/; stress the second syllable.\n- **obsidian** — /əb-SID-ee-ən/.\n- **crying obsidian** — /KRY-ing əb-SID-ee-ən/; keep the two words separate.\n- **Nether** — /NETH-ə/ in a non-rhotic delivery; use the creator’s natural final sound.\n- **respawn** — /ree-SPAWN/.\n- **glowstone** — /GLOH-stohn/.\n- **echo** — /EK-oh/; plural **echoes**, /EK-ohz/.\n- **Atlas** — /AT-ləs/. The full production name **Weeping Atlas** is /WEE-ping AT-ləs/.\n- **threshold** — /THRESH-hohld/.\n- **mouths** — /maʊðz/ in the phrase **one frame, many mouths**; use the voiced final sound.\n\n## Delivery and emphasis\n\n- **The map ended at the ridge.** — plain statement; do not imply danger yet.\n- **The wolf sat down first. That was vote enough. They could stay.** — warm but restrained. Use the identical recording in Scenes 2 and 13.\n- **Same cave. Same stone. Rain on the other side.** — observational pacing, with short pauses and no heavy stress on **rain**.\n- **Next mouth. Never previous.** — clear informational delivery, with light stress on **next** and **never**.\n- **Builder present at ignition becomes the key.** — Atlas insert only; if read during production discussion, stress **builder** and **key**.\n- **First threshold** — stress **first** only when contrasting it with the next mouth.\n- **At the first threshold, Moss was still beside him. That was enough for him to try.** — keep level and concise; the images carry the emotion.\n- No narration follows the creeper explosion.\n\n## Crew-only labels\n\n- `R0` to `R4` are pronounced **ar-zero** to **ar-four** in production discussion.\n- **MATCH PATH** and **MATCH FRAME A** are production labels and are not spoken.\n- **Immersive Portals** is the planned mod name in production discussion; it is not spoken in the story.\n- Crew labels do not appear in narration or on-screen story text.\n\n## Translation notes\n\nNo translated dialogue is currently planned. Preserve third-person perspective and the distinction between a conventional Nether portal, an unanchored echo mouth and the keyed final frame in any subtitles or localisation.\n"},{"path":".studio/internal/script/script-qc.md","content":"# Script QC — The Portal That Nobody Made\n\n> Complete the twelve specialised passes separately. Record machine-readable status in `revision-pass-status.json`; use this document for evidence, judgement, and required changes.\n\nScope of this record: textual review of `draft-02` only. It is not evidence of a table read, animatic, capture test, Minecraft mechanics test, mod test or audience response.\n\n## 1. Promise\n\nTextual review complete.\n\nScene 1 now promises a future emergency through smoke, urgent movement and unexplained purple cargo, then ends before the protagonist reaches the house. The same-camera jump places the protagonist and wolf on the equivalent R0 path and leads into ordinary early survival.\n\nThe portal remains the principal mystery. Its first reveal is strengthened visually because the Rain Echo is already visible through the impossible crying-obsidian frame. The main body still follows recognisable but incompatible versions of one ravine, and the ending still reveals the source connection and opening destination.\n\nThe cold open no longer states that Moss is in danger. That is an intentional consequence of the supplied scene boundary, while Scene 2 now establishes Moss through gameplay and naming before the portal journey.\n\n## 2. Causality\n\nTextual review complete.\n\nThe principal revised links are explicit:\n\n- the future smoke run supplies the image matched by the earlier R0 arrival;\n- choosing the clearing leads to woodcutting, shelter, crops, cows, fishing and naming Moss;\n- the unexplained hum interrupts established ordinary play and causes the ravine investigation;\n- the rainy destination visible through the frame makes its function concrete and causes entry;\n- the dry cave visible from R1 makes the attempted return reasonable because the surface remains outside the sightline;\n- the absent cobblestone causes a more deliberate marker test;\n- the second absent mark makes the Atlas’s first rule credible;\n- the collapsing route and failing mouth cause the final uncontrolled crossing;\n- incomplete materials cause R4 settlement and gradual preparation;\n- the added rug square supplies the proposed fire path;\n- the material requirement causes the supply absence;\n- sitting Moss at home prevents him following on the hazardous trip;\n- the empty room and failed search accelerate frame completion;\n- the Atlas requires the builder to be present when the charged frame is ignited;\n- a falling creeper interrupts the planned ignition and its blast kills the protagonist;\n- the blast reaching the charged frame causes the delayed R0 opening under the invented portal canon.\n\nThe fire spread, creeper damage, surviving geometry and charged-frame response remain proposed production links until tested or staged safely.\n\n## 3. Character agency\n\nTextual review complete.\n\nThe protagonist makes consequential decisions in choosing the clearing, establishing a home, investigating the hum, entering with Moss, attempting a return, marking the echoes, trusting only Atlas rules supported by observation, settling in R4, preparing cautiously, leaving Moss in the normally safer room and completing the frame after Moss disappears.\n\nThe revised death is not a deliberate activation blast. A creeper interrupts him before he lights the frame. His agency lies in being there and attempting the route; the fatal moment is an external Minecraft hazard. The narration and production language must not recast it as chosen suicide.\n\n## 4. Escalation\n\nTextual review complete.\n\nEscalation changes the problem rather than simply increasing spectacle:\n\n1. ordinary settlement establishes a readable baseline;\n2. an impossible window into a rainy version challenges that baseline;\n3. crossing creates displacement;\n4. the dry return view leads somewhere else;\n5. absent marks establish distinct echoes;\n6. the Atlas offers incomplete practical knowledge;\n7. the failed mouth creates entrapment;\n8. a chosen R4 home creates something meaningful to lose;\n9. Moss’s absence turns patient research into urgency;\n10. an ordinary hostile-mob accident ends the attempt before the successful route can be used.\n\nThe number of crossings remains limited to those that alter the audience’s model.\n\n## 5. Theme and character change\n\nTextual review complete.\n\nThe protagonist begins with ordinary survival competence and practical curiosity. He verifies portal behaviour before trusting the Atlas and takes time to build cover while R4 becomes home. Moss’s disappearance narrows that patience and prompts him to finish the frame.\n\nThe supplied creeper change alters the climax’s meaning. The protagonist no longer knowingly accepts a direct anchor blast; his attempt is cut short by a familiar Minecraft hazard. The portal still succeeds technically after he dies, but the tragedy is now interruption and bad timing rather than a deliberately accepted lethal cost.\n\nThe narration does not state a moral or imply that opening the route restores Moss.\n\n## 6. Information design\n\nTextual review complete.\n\n| Audience question | Fair evidence before resolution | Resolution or intended residue |\n|---|---|---|\n| Why does Scene 2 resemble Scene 1? | Exact path, camera and direction of travel; different terrain state | Scene 2 is the earlier R0 state of the same location |\n| When does the wolf become Moss? | He arrives as the protagonist’s tamed companion; a name tag is caught and labelled | The tag is applied on screen in Scene 2 |\n| Where does the first portal lead? | Rain, matching stone and parallax are visible through it | Crossing enters the Rain Echo already shown |\n| Why does returning fail? | From R1, only a dry cave is visible and the changed surface is hidden | Crossing reaches the dry Ash Echo; Atlas later confirms forward-only travel |\n| Are these changing worlds? | Same seam and coordinates; incompatible ecology; absent adjacent blocks | Marker tests establish stable, distinct echoes |\n| Who made the R0 portal connection? | Matched frame geometry, Atlas diagram and R4 materials | The protagonist constructs and keys the R4 source frame |\n| Why can the final frame reach R0? | First-threshold arrow, charged anchor and builder-at-ignition note | The keyed R4 frame targets the first clearing |\n| Why does the R0 entrant still reach R1? | Atlas note that unkeyed entry continues forwards | The earlier protagonist enters without the builder’s key |\n| What caused the house fire? | Open hearth, green rug and added nearest square | Scene 10 traces the proposed burn route into timber |\n| Did Moss die in R4? | Empty sitting place, failed search and no response | Intentionally unresolved; no false confirmation is supplied |\n| What kills the protagonist? | A plausible dark ledge is established above the ignition position | A creeper falls behind him and explodes before he lights the frame |\n| Why does the portal open afterwards? | Charged frame and ignition diagrams precede the blast | Under invented canon, the blast reaches and wakes the charged frame |\n| Did the protagonist survive? | Fade, explosion, absent avatar and scattered items | He dies; the later camera is explicitly omniscient presentation |\n| Who produced the older Atlas? | Matching journal, handwriting and final blank counterpart | Intentionally open reflective residue |\n\nThe seamless opening must always show the destination actually reached. Any contradictory view would break the audience contract.\n\n## 7. Scene necessity\n\nTextual review complete.\n\n- Scene 1 supplies future urgency and the path image matched in Scene 2.\n- Scene 2 establishes settlement, requested early-game actions, Moss’s name and the ordinary baseline.\n- Scene 3 presents the core contradiction and visible destination.\n- Scene 4 makes the failed return reasonable within the seamless-view design.\n- Scene 5 converts confusion into evidence.\n- Scene 6 supplies incomplete, tested knowledge.\n- Scene 7 changes travel into settlement.\n- Scene 8 earns the R4 home, relationship and fire setup.\n- Scene 9 causes separation and ignition.\n- Scene 10 completes the withheld emergency and changes the protagonist’s objective.\n- Scene 11 constructs the answer and establishes the ignition position and overhead route.\n- Scene 12 delivers the requested creeper death and delayed opening.\n- Scene 13 reveals the destination and reinterprets Scene 2.\n\nIf duration is excessive after a timed read, first shorten holds and repeated work actions. Retain the requested woodcutting, mob combat, cows, fishing, name-tag and naming beats in recognisable form.\n\n## 8. Dialogue, exposition, and subtext\n\nTextual review complete; spoken evidence pending.\n\nAll narrator lines are now third person. The language is more observational and less heightened, while the protagonist remains silent. No supporting voice has been added.\n\nAtlas exposition remains confined to short labels and deductions supported by earlier images. The narration no longer presents the death as a deliberate value declaration. The creeper is communicated through location, landing, hiss, fade and aftermath rather than explanation.\n\nThe repeated Scene 2 line returns unchanged in Scene 13. A spoken pass must verify that pronouns remain clear before the wolf receives the Moss name tag.\n\n## 9. Visual storytelling\n\nTextual review complete.\n\nPrimary visual carriers are:\n\n- the exact **MATCH PATH** composition linking R4 smoke to R0 arrival;\n- ordinary early-game actions establishing settlement;\n- the caught, labelled and applied Moss name tag;\n- seamless rain visible before the first crossing;\n- the lightning-shaped granite seam for geographic identity;\n- restricted portal sightlines supporting the mistaken return;\n- two missing constructed marks for the echo deduction;\n- matching journal repairs and handwriting for the Atlas connection;\n- ordinary and crying portal designs for system distinction;\n- Moss’s repeated rug choice and the added wool square for affection and danger;\n- dropped purple cargo for the later continuation;\n- matched crying-frame geometry for the builder reveal;\n- the dark overhead shelf, falling sound and close hiss for the creeper cause;\n- the exact clearing composition for the final reinterpretation.\n\nAn animatic must verify that these remain legible without crew labels or explanatory captions.\n\n## 10. Production feasibility\n\nScript-level textual review complete; execution evidence pending.\n\nThe draft uses one avatar, one wolf and one narrator. It avoids a visible counterpart and dialogue scene. The expanded early-game material can be captured as short actions around the R0 clearing rather than new dramatic sets.\n\nTesting or capture planning is still required for:\n\n- the exact same-camera R4-to-R0 path match;\n- concise woodcutting, mob, cow, fishing, anvil and name-tag coverage;\n- the selected Minecraft and Immersive Portals versions;\n- seamless destination alignment and replay or camera compatibility;\n- tamed-wolf entry and arrival blocking;\n- sitting behaviour during the supply absence;\n- the exact wool-to-timber fire route;\n- safe and readable falling-creeper staging;\n- the fatal blast plate and surviving frame geometry;\n- the fictional charged-frame response and delayed R0 view;\n- readable resets across five echoes;\n- the final through-ground transition.\n\nNone of these behaviours is recorded as tested by this document.\n\n## 11. Continuity and canon\n\nTextual review complete.\n\nDraft-02 consistently applies the latest supplied note:\n\n- Scene 1 stops after the protagonist drops the cargo and runs along the path;\n- Scene 2 begins from the same camera position in R0 and adds early survival settlement and Moss’s naming;\n- active crying portals show seamless destination views;\n- narration is grounded and third-person;\n- a falling creeper causes the protagonist’s death before ignition.\n\nThe R0–R4 chain, older journal, R4 rug fire, Moss’s unresolved absence, source-frame construction and R0 destination remain from the supplied Story proposal.\n\nThe unlocked Story document has not been revised in this task and still describes the shorter R0 arrival, first-person sample lines and direct respawn-anchor death. That is a known upstream alignment item, not a silent canon change or recorded lock.\n\nContinuity items requiring active tracking are **MATCH PATH**, **MATCH FRAME A**, the granite seam, portal-facing destination, cobblestone inventory, both journals, Moss’s name-tag state, green rug extension, conventional-portal distinction, supply cargo, broken wall, water direction, Atlas page damage, dark overhead ledge, frame block pattern and blast aftermath.\n\n## 12. Audience-results plan\n\nNo audience results are available.\n\nAfter release, investigate rather than assume:\n\n- whether the smoke-to-arrival match cut creates curiosity or confusion;\n- whether retention changes during the expanded early-game montage;\n- whether viewers recognise that Moss receives his name on screen;\n- whether comments show that the first portal visibly leads into rain;\n- whether the dry return view makes the R2 mistake understandable;\n- whether the Atlas sequence creates an information-load decline;\n- whether viewers understand the difference between keyed and unkeyed travel;\n- whether the creeper fall reads as a familiar gameplay accident rather than arbitrary editing;\n- whether viewers understand that the blast wakes the portal only under the story’s invented rules;\n- whether the final R0 destination creates a reveal response;\n- whether packaging promises the impossible portal rather than an ordinary survival montage.\n\nUse retention, comments and packaging results as evidence for hypotheses, not proof of a single cause. Inspect the corresponding edit before proposing revisions.\n"}],"questions":[],"warnings":["No spoken pass, table read, storyboard review, animatic, mechanics test or mod test is recorded; all such notes remain pending.","Immersive Portals compatibility, fishing/name-tag capture, the wool fire route, wolf transfer, falling-creeper staging and blast aftermath still require version-specific production validation.","The creeper blast waking the charged crying frame is invented portal canon, not vanilla Minecraft behaviour. The unlocked Story plan still describes the previous R0 sequence and direct-anchor death and is therefore out of alignment with this proposal."]}
{"summary":"Revised `draft-02` only as requested: Scene 1 now ends on the path and match-cuts into R0; Scene 2 adds grounded early-game settlement and Moss’s naming; portals use seamless destination views; narration is restrained and third-person; and Scene 12 replaces the deliberate anchor death with a falling creeper. Internal revision, spoken-pass, pronunciation and QC records are updated. The unlocked Story plan still needs later alignment with the revised R0 sequence and death mechanism.","document":"# Production script — The Portal That Nobody Made\n\n> Draft ID: `draft-02`. This revision follows the current unlocked echo-reality Story proposal and the latest script note. It records no creative lock or production validation.\n\n## Status and use\n\n- Editorial target: approximately 20 minutes. Scene timings are planning guides, not measured results or structural quotas.\n- `R0` to `R4` are crew labels and are never spoken.\n- One creator-voiced, third-person narrator follows the protagonist’s understanding. The on-screen protagonist does not speak.\n- Crying-obsidian portal behaviour is invented story canon. Seamless portal views are planned with the Immersive Portals mod; compatibility and capture behaviour have not been verified for the selected Minecraft version.\n- Minecraft fire, wolf, creeper and charged-anchor staging must be tested before capture.\n\n## Continuity keys\n\n- **MATCH PATH** is the same camera position, lens, path and direction of travel in Scenes 1 and 2. R4 shows the later smoke and developed landscape; R0 shows the fresh clearing.\n- Every ravine contains the same lightning-shaped granite seam. It is the first orientation anchor in each echo.\n- The protagonist’s survey journal begins mostly blank, with a stitched repair on one corner and a three-line ravine symbol. The Weeping Atlas is a weathered counterpart with the same repair, symbol and handwriting.\n- For crew continuity, the wolf is called Moss throughout. No in-world name appears above him until the Scene 2 name-tag beat.\n- Moss’s rug is moss-green wool. Its final added square sits nearest the open hearth and provides the proposed fire path.\n- Echo portals use crying obsidian, directional tear-like particles and a low layered hum. Their centres show seamless views of the destination rather than opaque purple fields.\n- The conventional Nether portal used for supply journeys has ordinary obsidian and familiar audio.\n- Moss’s R4 fate remains unseen. Show no body, collar drop or death message.\n- The final falling-creeper route needs a believable dark ledge above the activation position. The portal response to its blast reaching the charged frame is invented canon, not a claimed vanilla mechanic.\n\n---\n\n### 1. EXT. R4 RIDGE / HOMESTEAD PATH — NIGHT\n\n*Editorial target: 0:18*\n\n**Visual intent.** Open on smoke, urgent movement and unexplained purple cargo. End on a path composition that can jump directly to the same place in R0.\n\n**Audio intent.** Wind, hurried movement and distant fire. No narration and no music before the jump cut.\n\n**Action**\n\nNight. The protagonist crests the ridge from the direction of a conventional Nether portal. Crying obsidian and glowstone fill his pack and hands.\n\nHe sees smoke before he sees the house.\n\nHe drops the purple cargo and runs.\n\nFrom **MATCH PATH**, the camera watches him follow the path towards the smoke. He runs away from camera towards the homestead.\n\nJump cut on his movement into Scene 2.\n\n**Production note.** Preserve the exact camera position, lens, path line and direction of travel for Scene 2. Scene 10 resumes the emergency from the end of this shot.\n\n---\n\n### 2. EXT. R0 CLEARING ABOVE THE RAVINE — MORNING / PASSAGE OF DAYS\n\n*Editorial target: 2:45*\n\n**Visual intent.** Use the match cut to replace the later emergency with an ordinary arrival. Establish the protagonist and wolf through recognisable early-game routines before the portal interrupts them.\n\n**Audio intent.** The distant R4 fire cuts to morning wind, leaves and footsteps. Let work sounds carry the montage. Introduce the portal hum only near the end.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe jump cut keeps the exact **MATCH PATH** camera position. Morning replaces night. The developed R4 landscape is now the untouched R0 clearing, with no house or smoke.\n\nOn the same path, the protagonist walks towards the clearing with the wolf beside him. His equipment is simpler and his journal is mostly blank.\n\nTitle over the matched shot: **THE PORTAL THAT NOBODY MADE**\n\nAt the overlook, in a second locked composition marked **MATCH FRAME A**, the wolf checks the grass and sits. The protagonist unfolds a map whose explored edge ends at the ridge. He opens the clean survey journal, showing its stitched corner, and draws the three-line ravine symbol.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The map ended at the ridge. There were no paths or torches beyond it, and no one else used the world.\n\nThe wolf remains in the best patch of sun.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The wolf sat down first. That was vote enough. They could stay.\n\n**EARLY-GAME MONTAGE — SEVERAL MINECRAFT DAYS**\n\n- The protagonist cuts oak and birch, makes basic tools and raises a small temporary shelter.\n- At night, he kills a zombie and a skeleton near the unfinished walls, then clears a spider from the crop edge.\n- He plants wheat and later uses it to lead two cows into a simple fenced pen.\n- He fishes from the ravine stream. Ordinary catches fill a small chest.\n- On a later cast, a name tag comes up on the line.\n- After gathering enough iron for an anvil, he labels the tag **MOSS** and uses it on the wolf. The name appears above him for the first time.\n- The shelter gains a bed, a chest, a furnace and a small roof. The protagonist records the clearing and starter build in his journal.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The first days were ordinary. He cut wood, put up a roof, planted wheat and dealt with whatever reached the clearing after dark.\n\nThe newly named Moss follows him back from the anvil.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The tag said Moss. It suited him.\n\nHold on the compact shelter, cow pen, crops and fishing place. It is a beginning, not yet the later R4 house.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> A bed, a pen and a roof were enough for him to start calling the clearing home.\n\nWhile the protagonist marks a tree near the ravine edge, a low hum rises from below. Moss turns his head first. The protagonist stops cutting and listens.\n\nHe checks the cave area on his map. The surface is now familiar; the space beneath it remains blank.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> He knew the clearing by then. He had never opened the cave beneath it, and nothing he had built made that sound.\n\nHe packs the journal and starts down the ravine. Moss follows.\n\n**Production note.** Save **MATCH FRAME A**, including camera, lens, sun direction, equipment, Moss position and journal pose, for Scene 13. The fishing and name-tag beats are planned capture actions, not recorded survival or probability tests.\n\n---\n\n### 3. INT. R0 RAVINE CAVE — DAY\n\n*Editorial target: 1:35*\n\n**Visual intent.** Make the first sight of the portal both an authorship contradiction and a visible destination. The Rain Echo should already be clear through the frame.\n\n**Audio intent.** Let mining and paw sounds recede beneath the unfamiliar portal hum. Rain should be audible through the opening before the protagonist crosses.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe protagonist descends by water and short block placements. Moss waits at a narrow ledge until the route is safe.\n\nA lightning-shaped granite seam cuts across the cave wall.\n\nBeyond it stands a complete crying-obsidian frame. Tear-like particles move consistently towards its far face.\n\nThe centre is not an opaque violet surface. It is a seamless view into what first appears to be a mirror of the same cave, except rain falls through an opening in the ceiling on the other side while the R0 stone around the protagonist remains dry.\n\nThe camera moves laterally. The far view shifts with natural parallax. There is no reflected protagonist: the opening shows a real, rain-soaked version of the cave.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> It was crying obsidian, but it was not any portal he knew how to make. The opening already showed where it went.\n\nThe identical granite seam is visible beyond the frame. Rainwater crosses it and feeds a waterfall that does not exist behind the protagonist.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> Same cave. Same stone. Rain on the other side.\n\nThe protagonist circles the frame and checks the ceiling, floor and adjoining tunnels. There is no access shaft, scaffold, camp, dropped inventory or mining trail.\n\nHe compares the coordinates with his journal. The cave lies beneath the clearing he has now surveyed and settled, but he never opened a route to it.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> No one else used the world. There was no route into the cave, but somebody had placed every block.\n\nMoss approaches the opening, then looks back at the protagonist.\n\nThe protagonist checks the frame once more, removes Moss’s wait command and brings him beside the threshold.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> He checked it twice, brought Moss beside him and walked through.\n\nThey cross in one continuous movement. Dry cave audio gives way to rain as the camera passes the threshold.\n\n**Production note.** The planned Immersive Portals presentation must preserve the matched destination view and camera parallax. Mod version, multiplayer or replay compatibility, wolf transfer and capture stability remain unverified.\n\n---\n\n### 4. INT./EXT. R1 RAIN ECHO — DAY\n\n*Editorial target: 1:35*\n\n**Visual intent.** Confirm that the destination matches what was visible through the portal. Keep the geography recognisable while changing weather, growth and player history.\n\n**Audio intent.** Continue the rain already heard through the opening. Keep the same portal hum beneath it.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe protagonist and Moss complete their step into the Rain Echo beside the same lightning-shaped granite seam.\n\nRainwater pours through the cave roof. A waterfall occupies the route that was dry. Dense vegetation covers the surface. The R0 shelter, cow pen and surface marker are absent.\n\nThe protagonist checks his coordinates twice and overlays a sketch of the granite seam with the one in his journal.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The coordinates matched. The weather, water and tree growth did not.\n\nAt the surface, the ravine profile and distant tree line remain recognisable beneath their different growth.\n\nBack at the frame, the opening now shows a dry version of the cave. From this angle, the surface and its missing build are out of sight. The view resembles the R0 cave he left.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> From the wet side, the opening looked into a dry cave again. With the surface out of view, it looked like the way back.\n\nThe protagonist places one cobblestone block against the left foot of the R1 frame and draws its position in the journal.\n\nHe and Moss step through the dry view.\n\nThe camera crosses with them. On the far side, grey ash drifts through still air.\n\n---\n\n### 5. INT./EXT. R2 ASH ECHO TO R3 ARCHIVE ECHO — LATE DAY\n\n*Editorial target: 1:35*\n\n**Visual intent.** Turn the mistaken return into a practical test. The absent marks must remain readable without narration.\n\n**Audio intent.** Replace rain with dry wind and distant falling debris. Use a short musical pulse only when the second missing mark confirms the pattern.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe protagonist turns towards the left foot of the frame. The cobblestone block is absent.\n\nAt the surface, a naturally scorched tree line stands above an exposed ravine shelf. None of the R0 construction is present.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> He had stepped towards the dry cave he knew. The world beyond it was not his.\n\nHe searches the ground and checks the journal sketch. He then constructs a more deliberate mark: two cobblestone blocks in an L, with a torch on the outer block. He records the arrangement and remaining block count.\n\nThe portal opening shows a darker version of the cave fitted with an old survey recess. Supplies are thinning, and there is no safer route across the damaged surface.\n\nThe protagonist and Moss cross.\n\nIn the Archive Echo, the L-shaped mark is absent from the matching frame foot.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> One missing block could have been damage. Two different marks missing at the same coordinates were enough.\n\nHe writes a single word beneath the sketches: **ECHOES**.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The places shared a layout, but not a history. Each crossing had moved them to another one.\n\n**Production note.** Use identical close framing for both marks and both absences. Do not rely on colour grading alone to distinguish R1, R2 and R3.\n\n---\n\n### 6. INT. R3 ARCHIVE ECHO — DUSK\n\n*Editorial target: 2:00*\n\n**Visual intent.** Let the Atlas organise evidence already seen. Its final activation notes should remain incomplete and practical rather than becoming a lore lecture.\n\n**Audio intent.** Quiet the score for readable inserts. The seamless destination image begins stable, then skips and darkens as the mouth fails.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe survey recess is old but recognisably arranged by someone with the protagonist’s habits: torches on the same side, compact storage and the three-line ravine symbol cut into a desk.\n\nInside a protected drawer lies a weathered journal with the same stitched corner as the protagonist’s book.\n\nHe places the journals together. The shorthand, ravine symbol and arrow-shaped number seven match.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The repair and handwriting matched his journal. The pages did not.\n\n**ATLAS INSERTS — HOLD EACH LONG ENOUGH TO READ**\n\n1. Several plans of the same ravine laid over one another.\n2. A chain of portal mouths with every unanchored arrow pointing forwards: **NEXT MOUTH. NEVER PREVIOUS.**\n3. The phrase **ONE FRAME, MANY MOUTHS.**\n4. A complete crying-obsidian frame connected to a charged respawn anchor, with an ignition mark and a keyed arrow towards **FIRST THRESHOLD**.\n5. A note beside the receiving mouth: **UNKEYED ENTRY CONTINUES FORWARDS.**\n6. The warning **BUILDER PRESENT AT IGNITION BECOMES THE KEY.**\n7. A stone baffle and blast-distance diagram whose final measurement has been torn away.\n\nThe protagonist compares the Atlas arrows with the particles and his two absent marks.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The first diagrams matched what he had tested. An unkeyed crossing went to the next mouth, never the previous one.\n\nHe turns to the complete-frame diagram.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The last diagram was less certain. A complete frame, a charged anchor and its builder present at ignition could point towards the first threshold. The page with the safe distance was torn.\n\nThe view through the portal skips. One layer of the hum drops out. Gravel falls into the old surface stair and more shifts when the protagonist tries to clear it.\n\nMoss backs towards the frame. The protagonist looks from the blocked stair to the failing opening, takes both journals and chooses the remaining route.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The stair was blocked and the opening was failing. Forward was the only route still open.\n\nThey cross as the destination image contracts around them.\n\n**Production note.** The Atlas wording is the maximum required exposition. Do not add further portal explanation unless a later animatic demonstrates a specific comprehension failure.\n\n---\n\n### 7. INT./EXT. R4 HOME ECHO — MORNING\n\n*Editorial target: 1:15*\n\n**Visual intent.** Make arrival feel useful before revealing that the route has closed. Change the immediate objective from travelling to surviving.\n\n**Audio intent.** Begin with birds and stillness. Let the opening collapse softly rather than explode.\n\n**Action**\n\nThey emerge into a dry, habitable version of the ravine. The granite seam remains.\n\nBehind them, the destination image breaks apart. The projected upper blocks fade, leaving an incomplete set of crying blocks around empty air.\n\nThe protagonist tries ordinary fire. It burns briefly and goes out without opening a view.\n\nHe compares the broken mouth with the Atlas’s complete frame. He lacks the required crying obsidian, charged anchor and cover.\n\nAt the surface, there is clean water, timber and open ground. He plants a marker directly above the dead mouth.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> For a moment, the quiet was useful.\n\nHe checks the supplies remaining in his pack and begins a small shelter before nightfall.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The mouth had closed. He did not have the blocks or anchor to rebuild it, so he put shelter first.\n\n---\n\n### 8. EXT./INT. R4 HOMESTEAD — PASSAGE OF TIME\n\n*Editorial target: 2:30*\n\n**Visual intent.** Earn the R4 house and Moss’s importance through shared routines. Build the proposed material cause of the fire in plain sight.\n\n**Audio intent.** Use a restrained theme assembled from work sounds: block placement, furnace ticks, crops, rain and Moss’s movement. Leave room for quiet domestic holds.\n\n**Action — MONTAGE IN REPEATED LOCKED COMPOSITIONS**\n\n- The first shelter gains a floor, walls and a compact upper storey.\n- Moss repeatedly stands on the exact block the protagonist intends to place. The protagonist waits and leaves it until last.\n- Moss waits at a bridge while it is made safe, follows across and receives food after minor damage.\n- The protagonist builds a stone-backed open fireplace in the shared room.\n- Moss chooses the bare floor beside it. The protagonist lays a small moss-green wool rug beneath him.\n- During a storm, Moss goes directly to the rug while thunder sounds outside.\n- The protagonist keeps his current survey journal and the weathered Atlas side by side. His pages fill with echo diagrams and material counts.\n- A conventional obsidian Nether portal is built away from the ravine. Across several journeys, the protagonist returns with small amounts of crying obsidian and glowstone.\n- At the dead mouth, he measures space for a complete frame and sketches a stone baffle, but lights nothing.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> He called it a camp while the walls were temporary.\n\nThe house gains crops, storage and signs of use.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> Then the crops came in, Moss knew which doorway opened first, and the bed stayed where it was. It had become a house.\n\nMoss occupies a construction block. The protagonist lowers his tool and waits.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> Moss often stood on the next block. He learnt to place that one last.\n\nAt the hearth, Moss lies with his front paws beyond the rug’s edge. The protagonist adds one square of moss-green wool between the old rug and the warmth.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> Moss always chose the warm edge. He added one more square of rug.\n\nHold on the new wool square near the open flame. Stone surrounds the hearth, but wool now provides a proposed path towards the timber floor and supports.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The fireplace was stone-backed. He checked the stone and missed how close the wool now sat to the flame.\n\nThe protagonist checks a material diagram. Only the final crying blocks and glowstone remain uncollected.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> He gathered the Atlas materials a little at a time. The frame could wait until he had enough cover.\n\n**Production note.** The final rug extension is the proposed causal change. Its distance and route into timber require version-specific testing before the set is approved.\n\n---\n\n### 9. INT. R4 HOMESTEAD / CONVENTIONAL PORTAL — DAY\n\n*Editorial target: 1:10*\n\n**Visual intent.** Make leaving Moss an established safety decision. End on the unnoticed material consequence.\n\n**Audio intent.** Keep the domestic theme warm but spare. After the conventional portal closes, isolate the hearth crackle.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe protagonist packs for the final supply journey. Moss stands to follow.\n\nHe checks the stone hearth, feeds Moss and gives the sit command on the green rug. Moss settles on the newly added square.\n\nThe protagonist leaves through the conventional obsidian portal.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The final run was for the last crying blocks and enough glowstone to charge the anchor. After that, he planned to measure again before lighting anything.\n\nMoss remains beside the hearth.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> Moss came on ordinary journeys. The Nether was not one of them. The house looked safer.\n\nThe conventional portal sound fades.\n\nHold on the open hearth and nearest wool square. Fire appears on its edge. Moss remains sitting as the first flame spreads out of frame.\n\nCut before the room is engulfed.\n\n**Production note.** Do not imply this spread has been verified. If the selected version cannot reproduce the planned wool-to-timber route, revise the geometry with human approval while preserving the visible domestic cause.\n\n---\n\n### 10. EXT./INT. R4 HOMESTEAD AND SEARCH ROUTES — NIGHT TO DAWN\n\n*Editorial target: 2:35*\n\n**Visual intent.** Resume the emergency exactly where Scene 1 stopped. Reveal an empty sitting place rather than a graphic answer, then let the failed search accelerate the frame decision.\n\n**Audio intent.** Continue Scene 1’s wind, footsteps and fire. After the wall breaks, suppress music and use distant fire, footsteps and pauses for possible returning paws.\n\n**Action**\n\nResume the **MATCH PATH** run from Scene 1. The protagonist follows the path to the burning house.\n\nA storage window breaks outward. He passes recoverable chests without looking at them.\n\nAt the shared-room doorway, flame has reached the moss-green wool beside the stone fireplace. Moss is not visible.\n\nThe protagonist empties his water bucket through the doorway. Steam fills the opening, but burning timber falls across the direct route.\n\nHe turns, breaks through the shortest plank wall and enters through the smoke.\n\nThe shared room is empty. The stone fireplace remains, but the green rug has burned away. A charred fan leads from its position into the timber floor and wall. Moss’s sitting place is bare.\n\nShow no body, collar drop or message.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The hearth remained. The rug did not. The burn started where the extra square had been and travelled into the timber.\n\nThe protagonist moves the dropped crying obsidian and glowstone out of the fire path, then searches.\n\nHe checks behind surviving furniture, the crop field, ridge, conventional portal shelter and ravine route. He carries Moss’s food and stops repeatedly to listen.\n\nAt the storm shelter beneath the window, he finds nothing.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> He checked the crops, the ridge, the ravine path and the place Moss used during storms. Nothing answered.\n\nA long still hold. Only fire and morning birds remain.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> There was no collar, no message and no proof of what had happened. Moss was simply gone.\n\nAt dawn, the protagonist returns to the ruins. He sees the recovered crying obsidian beside the weathered Atlas.\n\nHe opens the page marked **FIRST THRESHOLD**. A small drawing of Moss appears beside the clean R0 journal sketch.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The Atlas marked one route as the first threshold. It was the last place where he had seen Moss alive.\n\nHe looks towards the ravine.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> He could keep searching or spend more time testing the frame. By morning, he had decided to finish it.\n\nHe takes the Atlas, his journal, the crying obsidian and the glowstone. The ruined house continues to burn behind him as he descends.\n\n---\n\n### 11. INT. R4 RAVINE — DAY TO NIGHT\n\n*Editorial target: 1:45*\n\n**Visual intent.** Answer the construction mystery through physical work. Establish the partial cover, ignition position and overhead route needed for the creeper death.\n\n**Audio intent.** Build rhythm from block placement and stone work. Introduce the original portal hum only as a faint memory in the score.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe protagonist uses the granite seam, surface marker and coordinates to locate the dead mouth exactly.\n\nHe fills its missing positions and constructs a complete crying-obsidian frame. Its dimensions and block imperfections match the R0 portal.\n\nHe places a respawn anchor according to the Atlas diagram and charges it with glowstone. The frame remains dark.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The granite seam and coordinates placed the dead mouth exactly where the Atlas said it would be.\n\nHe places the final crying block.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The frame was made from blocks he had gathered. Only its destination was unusual.\n\n**ATLAS INSERT**\n\nThe keyed arrow runs from the full frame to **FIRST THRESHOLD**. At the far mouth, the unkeyed arrow continues into the ordinary forward chain.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> If the diagram was right, lighting this frame would project the mouth beneath the first clearing. His earlier crossing would still continue forwards because he had entered without the builder’s key.\n\nThe protagonist builds a thick stone baffle offset from the frame. He tests whether he can reach its inside edge with flint and steel while remaining behind most of the cover.\n\nThe torn safety page removes the final distance measurement.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The builder had to be present at ignition. The page showed cover, but the measurement that set it back from the frame was missing.\n\nHe adds the thickest blocks that still permit him to reach the opening.\n\nThe floor and frame are lit with torches. High behind the working position, a recessed shelf remains dark and partly hidden by the ravine wall.\n\nNight settles above the ravine. The protagonist puts both journals behind the baffle, equips flint and steel and faces the frame.\n\n**Production note.** The dark shelf and drop line must make the creeper’s arrival spatially plausible without turning it into an early reveal. Validate safe capture blocking separately from the story outcome.\n\n---\n\n### 12. INT. R4 RAVINE — NIGHT\n\n*Editorial target: 1:05*\n\n**Visual intent.** Let an ordinary Minecraft hazard interrupt the planned ignition. Keep the protagonist facing the frame, then withhold the impact visually and continue after his death.\n\n**Audio intent.** Remove the score before ignition. The falling-creeper landing, close hiss and explosion carry the death. Afterward, use muffled silence, debris and the gradual portal hum.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe protagonist checks the Atlas drawing of the first threshold, the charged anchor and the baffle.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> The frame was complete. The anchor was charged. The torn diagram gave him no better measurement.\n\nA brief visual memory: Moss in the R0 sun, Moss occupying a construction block and Moss on the rug.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O.)**\n\n> At the first threshold, Moss was still beside him. That was enough for him to try.\n\nThe protagonist steps into the position that lets him reach the frame and raises the flint and steel.\n\nA heavy landing sounds on the stone directly behind him. The close ignition hiss of a creeper follows at once.\n\nHe does not turn around.\n\nHe closes his eyes.\n\nFade to black.\n\nThe creeper explodes.\n\nNo narration follows his death.\n\nFor several seconds, nothing happens.\n\nThe picture returns to a locked exterior plate. Smoke and falling debris fill the ravine. The crying frame still stands. The protagonist is absent and his equipped items are scattered.\n\nThe charged anchor gives one brief violet flash inside the smoke.\n\nOne tear-like particle crosses the frame. Then another. Purple light gathers along the crying blocks, and the empty centre resolves into a clear, seamless view of the corresponding R0 cave a fraction too late.\n\nThe original low hum returns.\n\n**Production note.** The creeper fall, fatal blast, item scatter, surviving frame, anchor flash and delayed destination view may require separate controlled plates. A creeper blast activating the charged echo frame is invented story canon and has not been demonstrated as vanilla behaviour.\n\n---\n\n### 13. THROUGH THE FRAME / R0 CLEARING — MORNING\n\n*Editorial target: 1:00*\n\n**Visual intent.** Reveal the apparent beginning as the destination. The seamless portal view should connect R4 to R0 before the repeated surface composition changes its meaning.\n\n**Audio intent.** Carry the portal hum through the crossing, then reuse the exact Scene 2 narration take and morning ambience.\n\n**Action**\n\nThe camera remains after the protagonist’s death. It approaches the active R4 frame as omniscient audience access, not as his viewpoint or surviving consciousness.\n\nThe R0 cave is already visible through the opening. The camera crosses into it, follows the matching granite seam and rises through stylised layers of dark stone and soil.\n\nIt reaches the surface in **MATCH FRAME A**.\n\nMorning. The earlier protagonist opens his mostly blank survey journal. Its stitched corner and three-line ravine symbol are clear. Moss sits at the overlook, before receiving his on-screen name tag later in Scene 2.\n\n**NARRATOR (V.O. — IDENTICAL RECORDING FROM SCENE 2)**\n\n> The wolf sat down first. That was vote enough. They could stay.\n\nThe low portal hum rises from beneath them.\n\nThe protagonist pauses and turns towards the ravine.\n\nCut underground to the complete active crying-obsidian mouth waiting in the cave. Through its seamless centre, rain falls in R1.\n\nCut to black.\n\n**Production note.** The through-ground move may use a purpose-built vertical set and matched dissolves. It must not imply that the dead protagonist or his consciousness crosses the portal.\n\n**End.**\n","files":[{"path":".studio/internal/script/revision-pass-status.json","content":"{\n  \"schema_version\": 2,\n  \"draft_id\": \"draft-02\",\n  \"passes\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"promise\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Scenes 1–3 now connect the future smoke to an ordinary R0 arrival, establish early survival life with Moss and reveal the rainy destination through the impossible portal before entry. Scenes 4–13 retain the cumulative echo evidence, domestic loss, final construction and opening destination.\",\n      \"decision\": \"For draft-02, retain the matched path cut, early-game settlement, seamless rain reveal and delayed builder pay-off without adding an explanatory character.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"causality\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Settlement makes the underground hum interrupt ordinary play; the visible rainy destination causes investigation; a dry cave view makes the attempted return reasonable; marker failures make the Atlas credible; the failed mouth causes settlement; material gathering causes Moss to remain home; the rug supplies the proposed fire path; loss accelerates frame completion; and the falling creeper causes the fatal blast that reaches the charged frame before its delayed activation.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Retain these links and validate the seamless sightlines, rug fire, creeper fall, blast result and fictional charged-frame response before capture.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"dialogue_and_subtext\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"A textual revision changed every narrator passage to restrained third-person language, kept the protagonist silent and retained concise Atlas labels. No spoken performance evidence is claimed.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Keep the narrator external but close to the protagonist’s available understanding. Test breath, natural delivery and dramatic restraint in the pending spoken pass.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"visual_storytelling\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The matched path, early-game actions, on-screen Moss name tag, seamless rainy view, granite seam, absent marks, paired journals, rug extension, dropped cargo, falling-creeper sound beat, frame geometry and repeated clearing composition carry the principal information visually.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Use locked match shots and readable destination views; do not rely on narration or colour grading alone to distinguish echoes.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"production_feasibility\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The textual feasibility pass retains one avatar, one wolf and one narrator; adds a bounded early-game montage; plans seamless views through Immersive Portals; and permits controlled plates for wolf crossings, fire, the creeper fall, blast aftermath and final transition.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Proceed to storyboard and version-specific tests before capture. Treat mod compatibility, name-tag capture, fire spread, wolf transfer, creeper blocking and final activation as unverified.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"continuity_and_canon\",\n      \"required\": true,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Draft-02 consistently uses third-person narration, introduces Moss’s on-screen name in R0, presents every active echo mouth as a seamless destination view and attributes the protagonist’s death to a falling creeper. The R0–R4 chain, Weeping Atlas, R4 rug fire and R0 destination remain intact.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Treat the latest human script note as governing this unlocked draft. Record that the unlocked Story document still describes the earlier R0 arrival and fatal direct-anchor activation and will need separate alignment if approved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"character_agency\",\n      \"required\": false,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The protagonist chooses the clearing, establishes a survival home, investigates the hum, enters with Moss, tests successive echoes, settles in R4, prepares the frame gradually and decides to attempt ignition after Moss disappears. The creeper, rather than a deliberate self-detonation, causes his death.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Preserve his agency in choosing and preparing the attempt, but do not describe the creeper death as a chosen suicide or successful personal crossing.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"escalation\",\n      \"required\": false,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Ordinary settlement becomes an impossible visible destination, then displacement, failed return, distinct echoes, incomplete knowledge and entrapment. Domestic stability becomes loss, and the final technical attempt is interrupted by an ordinary hostile-mob hazard.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Keep each echo tied to a changed understanding and keep the creeper beat brief rather than expanding it into a separate threat sequence.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"theme_and_change\",\n      \"required\": false,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The protagonist moves from routine survival and evidence gathering towards a hurried attempt after Moss’s disappearance. The revised climax ends in an accidental Minecraft death while the technical route still opens too late.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Keep the narration grounded and avoid turning the accident into a spoken moral or implying that the portal restores Moss.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"information\",\n      \"required\": false,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The rainy destination is visible before entry; limited cave sightlines make the dry return view plausibly misleading; absent marks establish separate echoes; Atlas diagrams clarify keyed and unkeyed travel; Scene 11 establishes the dark overhead route; and the blast aftermath confirms death before the R0 view appears.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Retain the unkeyed-entry note and ensure the seamless view always depicts the destination actually reached.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"scene_necessity\",\n      \"required\": false,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Scene 1 now supplies the future emergency and match cut only. Scene 2 establishes settlement, early gameplay and Moss’s name before the portal. The remaining scenes retain their existing functions, while Scenes 11–12 now prepare and deliver the falling-creeper outcome.\",\n      \"decision\": \"If timing grows after a spoken or animatic pass, compress individual montage shots without removing the requested woodcutting, mob combat, cows, fishing, name tag or Moss-naming beats.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"audience_results_plan\",\n      \"required\": false,\n      \"status\": \"complete\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The QC record defines questions for the new match cut, early-game montage, seamless destination comprehension, third-person narration and creeper interruption. No audience result or causal claim is recorded.\",\n      \"decision\": \"Use future audience evidence to form hypotheses, then inspect the corresponding edit and comments before proposing changes.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n"},{"path":".studio/internal/script/table-read-notes.md","content":"# Table-read / spoken-pass notes — The Portal That Nobody Made\n\n## Method and participants\n\nNo solo spoken read, multi-voice table read, storyboard read or animatic review has been conducted or supplied for `draft-02`.\n\nParticipants: none recorded.\n\nPlanned evidence: a solo spoken read for breath, tone and provisional duration, followed by a storyboard or animatic review focused on the Scene 1–2 match cut, seamless portal sightlines, echo geography and final activation logic.\n\n## Clarity failures\n\nNo observed spoken-read failures can be recorded yet.\n\nItems to test:\n\n- whether the same-camera jump from the R4 run to the R0 walk is immediately readable;\n- whether viewers understand that the wolf is the same companion before and after the on-screen Moss name tag;\n- whether the early-game montage feels like settlement rather than an unrelated gameplay compilation;\n- whether the rainy view through the first frame clearly represents the destination before entry;\n- whether the restricted cave view makes the mistaken return to a dry echo reasonable;\n- whether R1, R2 and R3 remain distinct without crew labels;\n- whether viewers understand that the conventional Nether portal is unrelated to the crying frame;\n- whether keyed R4-to-R0 travel and unkeyed R0-to-R1 travel remain understandable;\n- whether the falling-creeper audio, fade and aftermath clearly communicate the protagonist’s death;\n- whether Moss’s R4 fate reads as intentionally unknown rather than accidentally omitted.\n\n## Rhythm, breath, and repetitive dialogue\n\nNo performance evidence is available.\n\nTest whether the expanded Scene 2 montage delays the portal beyond the intended audience promise. Listen especially to the Atlas explanation in Scene 6, the route explanation in Scene 11 and the search narration in Scene 10.\n\nThe narration should sound observational and grounded. Flag any delivery that restores the heightened first-person tone removed in this revision.\n\n## Emotional transitions\n\nNo performed transition has been assessed.\n\nTest the movement from the smoke run into the calm R0 walk, the naming of Moss, the later R4 domestic montage, the pause after the failed search and the abrupt creeper interruption. The final death should read as an ordinary hazard interrupting a consequential attempt, not as a deliberate self-detonation.\n\n## Geography and visual comprehension\n\nNo storyboard or animatic evidence is available.\n\nThe review should track **MATCH PATH**, **MATCH FRAME A**, the lightning-shaped granite seam, portal orientation, destination visible through each frame, left-foot marks, surface marker and vertical relationship between cave and clearing.\n\nConfirm that the view shown through each seamless portal matches the echo reached after crossing. Use a simple route map during review if any transition depends on narration for orientation.\n\n## Exposition and subtext\n\nNo spoken exposition pass has occurred.\n\nCheck whether third-person references remain unambiguous, particularly before Moss receives his name. Verify that narration does not repeat information already visible through the portals or in the Atlas inserts.\n\nThe Atlas labels are intentionally concise. The creeper should not require advance narration; the dark overhead route and sound cue should provide the necessary spatial cause.\n\n## Production difficulty exposed by the read\n\nA read has not exposed production difficulty.\n\nKnown script-level risks to examine during storyboard and capture planning are the exact path match, early-game montage length, fishing and name-tag staging, Immersive Portals compatibility, wolf portal blocking, five readable ravine states, reproducible fire geometry, the falling-creeper route, blast aftermath and final through-ground transition.\n\n## Changes made and unresolved decisions\n\nNo changes are attributed to a table read or spoken pass.\n\nThis revision implements the supplied note by ending Scene 1 on the path, matching it to the R0 arrival, expanding Scene 2 with ordinary early gameplay and Moss’s naming, showing destinations seamlessly through the crying frames, changing narration to restrained third person and replacing the direct-anchor death with a falling creeper.\n\nUnresolved evidence includes the target Minecraft edition and version, compatible Immersive Portals version, validated portal sightlines, wolf transfer, name-tag capture method, fire geometry, creeper fall and damage blocking, fictional charged-frame activation, and any duration trims identified by the pending read.\n"},{"path":".studio/internal/script/pronunciation-guide.md","content":"# Pronunciation and language guide\n\n- Delivery accent: creator’s natural English; do not imitate an accent.\n- Spelling convention: British English.\n- Narration perspective: external third person, kept close to what the protagonist can observe or infer.\n- Delivery tone: grounded and conversational. Do not add heightened drama that is absent from the wording.\n- These cues support consistency only; the creator’s natural, intelligible delivery takes priority.\n\n## Names and terms\n\n- **Moss** — /mɒs/; short vowel, like the plant.\n- **ravine** — /rə-VEE-n/; stress the second syllable.\n- **obsidian** — /əb-SID-ee-ən/.\n- **crying obsidian** — /KRY-ing əb-SID-ee-ən/; keep the two words separate.\n- **Nether** — /NETH-ə/ in a non-rhotic delivery; use the creator’s natural final sound.\n- **respawn** — /ree-SPAWN/.\n- **glowstone** — /GLOH-stohn/.\n- **echo** — /EK-oh/; plural **echoes**, /EK-ohz/.\n- **Atlas** — /AT-ləs/. The full production name **Weeping Atlas** is /WEE-ping AT-ləs/.\n- **threshold** — /THRESH-hohld/.\n- **mouths** — /maʊðz/ in the phrase **one frame, many mouths**; use the voiced final sound.\n\n## Delivery and emphasis\n\n- **The map ended at the ridge.** — plain statement; do not imply danger yet.\n- **The wolf sat down first. That was vote enough. They could stay.** — warm but restrained. Use the identical recording in Scenes 2 and 13.\n- **Same cave. Same stone. Rain on the other side.** — observational pacing, with short pauses and no heavy stress on **rain**.\n- **Next mouth. Never previous.** — clear informational delivery, with light stress on **next** and **never**.\n- **Builder present at ignition becomes the key.** — Atlas insert only; if read during production discussion, stress **builder** and **key**.\n- **First threshold** — stress **first** only when contrasting it with the next mouth.\n- **At the first threshold, Moss was still beside him. That was enough for him to try.** — keep level and concise; the images carry the emotion.\n- No narration follows the creeper explosion.\n\n## Crew-only labels\n\n- `R0` to `R4` are pronounced **ar-zero** to **ar-four** in production discussion.\n- **MATCH PATH** and **MATCH FRAME A** are production labels and are not spoken.\n- **Immersive Portals** is the planned mod name in production discussion; it is not spoken in the story.\n- Crew labels do not appear in narration or on-screen story text.\n\n## Translation notes\n\nNo translated dialogue is currently planned. Preserve third-person perspective and the distinction between a conventional Nether portal, an unanchored echo mouth and the keyed final frame in any subtitles or localisation.\n"},{"path":".studio/internal/script/script-qc.md","content":"# Script QC — The Portal That Nobody Made\n\n> Complete the twelve specialised passes separately. Record machine-readable status in `revision-pass-status.json`; use this document for evidence, judgement, and required changes.\n\nScope of this record: textual review of `draft-02` only. It is not evidence of a table read, animatic, capture test, Minecraft mechanics test, mod test or audience response.\n\n## 1. Promise\n\nTextual review complete.\n\nScene 1 now promises a future emergency through smoke, urgent movement and unexplained purple cargo, then ends before the protagonist reaches the house. The same-camera jump places the protagonist and wolf on the equivalent R0 path and leads into ordinary early survival.\n\nThe portal remains the principal mystery. Its first reveal is strengthened visually because the Rain Echo is already visible through the impossible crying-obsidian frame. The main body still follows recognisable but incompatible versions of one ravine, and the ending still reveals the source connection and opening destination.\n\nThe cold open no longer states that Moss is in danger. That is an intentional consequence of the supplied scene boundary, while Scene 2 now establishes Moss through gameplay and naming before the portal journey.\n\n## 2. Causality\n\nTextual review complete.\n\nThe principal revised links are explicit:\n\n- the future smoke run supplies the image matched by the earlier R0 arrival;\n- choosing the clearing leads to woodcutting, shelter, crops, cows, fishing and naming Moss;\n- the unexplained hum interrupts established ordinary play and causes the ravine investigation;\n- the rainy destination visible through the frame makes its function concrete and causes entry;\n- the dry cave visible from R1 makes the attempted return reasonable because the surface remains outside the sightline;\n- the absent cobblestone causes a more deliberate marker test;\n- the second absent mark makes the Atlas’s first rule credible;\n- the collapsing route and failing mouth cause the final uncontrolled crossing;\n- incomplete materials cause R4 settlement and gradual preparation;\n- the added rug square supplies the proposed fire path;\n- the material requirement causes the supply absence;\n- sitting Moss at home prevents him following on the hazardous trip;\n- the empty room and failed search accelerate frame completion;\n- the Atlas requires the builder to be present when the charged frame is ignited;\n- a falling creeper interrupts the planned ignition and its blast kills the protagonist;\n- the blast reaching the charged frame causes the delayed R0 opening under the invented portal canon.\n\nThe fire spread, creeper damage, surviving geometry and charged-frame response remain proposed production links until tested or staged safely.\n\n## 3. Character agency\n\nTextual review complete.\n\nThe protagonist makes consequential decisions in choosing the clearing, establishing a home, investigating the hum, entering with Moss, attempting a return, marking the echoes, trusting only Atlas rules supported by observation, settling in R4, preparing cautiously, leaving Moss in the normally safer room and completing the frame after Moss disappears.\n\nThe revised death is not a deliberate activation blast. A creeper interrupts him before he lights the frame. His agency lies in being there and attempting the route; the fatal moment is an external Minecraft hazard. The narration and production language must not recast it as chosen suicide.\n\n## 4. Escalation\n\nTextual review complete.\n\nEscalation changes the problem rather than simply increasing spectacle:\n\n1. ordinary settlement establishes a readable baseline;\n2. an impossible window into a rainy version challenges that baseline;\n3. crossing creates displacement;\n4. the dry return view leads somewhere else;\n5. absent marks establish distinct echoes;\n6. the Atlas offers incomplete practical knowledge;\n7. the failed mouth creates entrapment;\n8. a chosen R4 home creates something meaningful to lose;\n9. Moss’s absence turns patient research into urgency;\n10. an ordinary hostile-mob accident ends the attempt before the successful route can be used.\n\nThe number of crossings remains limited to those that alter the audience’s model.\n\n## 5. Theme and character change\n\nTextual review complete.\n\nThe protagonist begins with ordinary survival competence and practical curiosity. He verifies portal behaviour before trusting the Atlas and takes time to build cover while R4 becomes home. Moss’s disappearance narrows that patience and prompts him to finish the frame.\n\nThe supplied creeper change alters the climax’s meaning. The protagonist no longer knowingly accepts a direct anchor blast; his attempt is cut short by a familiar Minecraft hazard. The portal still succeeds technically after he dies, but the tragedy is now interruption and bad timing rather than a deliberately accepted lethal cost.\n\nThe narration does not state a moral or imply that opening the route restores Moss.\n\n## 6. Information design\n\nTextual review complete.\n\n| Audience question | Fair evidence before resolution | Resolution or intended residue |\n|---|---|---|\n| Why does Scene 2 resemble Scene 1? | Exact path, camera and direction of travel; different terrain state | Scene 2 is the earlier R0 state of the same location |\n| When does the wolf become Moss? | He arrives as the protagonist’s tamed companion; a name tag is caught and labelled | The tag is applied on screen in Scene 2 |\n| Where does the first portal lead? | Rain, matching stone and parallax are visible through it | Crossing enters the Rain Echo already shown |\n| Why does returning fail? | From R1, only a dry cave is visible and the changed surface is hidden | Crossing reaches the dry Ash Echo; Atlas later confirms forward-only travel |\n| Are these changing worlds? | Same seam and coordinates; incompatible ecology; absent adjacent blocks | Marker tests establish stable, distinct echoes |\n| Who made the R0 portal connection? | Matched frame geometry, Atlas diagram and R4 materials | The protagonist constructs and keys the R4 source frame |\n| Why can the final frame reach R0? | First-threshold arrow, charged anchor and builder-at-ignition note | The keyed R4 frame targets the first clearing |\n| Why does the R0 entrant still reach R1? | Atlas note that unkeyed entry continues forwards | The earlier protagonist enters without the builder’s key |\n| What caused the house fire? | Open hearth, green rug and added nearest square | Scene 10 traces the proposed burn route into timber |\n| Did Moss die in R4? | Empty sitting place, failed search and no response | Intentionally unresolved; no false confirmation is supplied |\n| What kills the protagonist? | A plausible dark ledge is established above the ignition position | A creeper falls behind him and explodes before he lights the frame |\n| Why does the portal open afterwards? | Charged frame and ignition diagrams precede the blast | Under invented canon, the blast reaches and wakes the charged frame |\n| Did the protagonist survive? | Fade, explosion, absent avatar and scattered items | He dies; the later camera is explicitly omniscient presentation |\n| Who produced the older Atlas? | Matching journal, handwriting and final blank counterpart | Intentionally open reflective residue |\n\nThe seamless opening must always show the destination actually reached. Any contradictory view would break the audience contract.\n\n## 7. Scene necessity\n\nTextual review complete.\n\n- Scene 1 supplies future urgency and the path image matched in Scene 2.\n- Scene 2 establishes settlement, requested early-game actions, Moss’s name and the ordinary baseline.\n- Scene 3 presents the core contradiction and visible destination.\n- Scene 4 makes the failed return reasonable within the seamless-view design.\n- Scene 5 converts confusion into evidence.\n- Scene 6 supplies incomplete, tested knowledge.\n- Scene 7 changes travel into settlement.\n- Scene 8 earns the R4 home, relationship and fire setup.\n- Scene 9 causes separation and ignition.\n- Scene 10 completes the withheld emergency and changes the protagonist’s objective.\n- Scene 11 constructs the answer and establishes the ignition position and overhead route.\n- Scene 12 delivers the requested creeper death and delayed opening.\n- Scene 13 reveals the destination and reinterprets Scene 2.\n\nIf duration is excessive after a timed read, first shorten holds and repeated work actions. Retain the requested woodcutting, mob combat, cows, fishing, name-tag and naming beats in recognisable form.\n\n## 8. Dialogue, exposition, and subtext\n\nTextual review complete; spoken evidence pending.\n\nAll narrator lines are now third person. The language is more observational and less heightened, while the protagonist remains silent. No supporting voice has been added.\n\nAtlas exposition remains confined to short labels and deductions supported by earlier images. The narration no longer presents the death as a deliberate value declaration. The creeper is communicated through location, landing, hiss, fade and aftermath rather than explanation.\n\nThe repeated Scene 2 line returns unchanged in Scene 13. A spoken pass must verify that 